# Exclude dependencies from dependency analysis

A project's dependencies can be analyzed as part of the build process by binding the `dependency:analyze-only` goal to the lifecycle. By default, the analysis will be performed during the `verify` lifecycle phase. 

It is possible to have necessary dependencies on the classpath that cause either "Declared but unused" or "Undeclared but used" warnings. One common cause of byte code analysis being unable to determine whether a jar is required are annotations with source retention. Another common cause is a class that is loaded by reflection at runtime.

The dependency plugin does not warn about a few common dependencies where its analysis is known to be unreliable, most notably SLF4J.

If you encounter other false positives, you can configure the plugin to ignore particular dependencies that are "declared but unused", "undeclared but used", and "non-test scoped". See the following POM configuration for an example:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>analyze</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>analyze-only</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <failOnWarning>true</failOnWarning>

              <!-- ignore jsr305 for "used but undeclared", "declared but unused", and "non-test scoped" -->
              <ignoredDependencies>
                <ignoredDependency>com.google.code.findbugs:jsr305</ignoredDependency>
              </ignoredDependencies>

              <!-- ignore annotations for "used but undeclared" warnings -->
              <ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependencies>
                <ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependency>com.google.code.findbugs:annotations</ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependency>
              </ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependencies>

              <!-- ignore annotations for "unused but declared" warnings -->
              <ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependencies>
                <ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependency>com.google.code.findbugs:annotations</ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependency>
              </ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependencies>

              <!-- ignore annotations for "non-test scoped" warnings -->
              <ignoredNonTestScopedDependencies>
                <ignoredNonTestScopedDependency>com.google.code.findbugs:annotations</ignoredNonTestScopedDependency>
              </ignoredNonTestScopedDependencies>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  ...
</project>
```

Each ignore pattern has the form `[groupId]:[artifactId]:[type]:[version]:[scope]`. Every segment is optional and supports full and partial `*` wildcards, and the `scope` segment is available since 3.12.0. For example, `org.acme:::*:runtime` ignores only the runtime scoped dependencies of group `org.acme`. An artifact that matches `ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependencies` is also left out of the "non-test scoped" warnings, because it is deliberately not declared.

The four lists can also be set on the command line, as comma-separated patterns, through the `mdep.analyze.ignoredDependencies`, `mdep.analyze.ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependencies`, `mdep.analyze.ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependencies` and `mdep.analyze.ignoredNonTestScopedDependencies` properties. The `dependency:analyze-dep-mgt` goal accepts the same patterns in its own `ignoredDependencies` parameter and skips the matching artifacts.

## Dependencies that bytecode analysis cannot see

The analysis reads class references from the compiled classes only. A dependency that the code reaches in another way is reported as "declared but unused", even though removing it breaks the build or the application at runtime. Typical cases are:

* `ServiceLoader` (SPI) providers, such as a JDBC driver, a logging backend or a transport implementation, that are found through `META-INF/services`.
* Sisu and Plexus components, which are found through `META-INF/sisu` or `META-INF/plexus/components.xml`.
* Test engines and test infrastructure, such as `junit-jupiter-engine` or a Surefire provider, that the test runner loads by name.
* Runtime-only libraries, such as a driver that is selected by a configuration value.

Keep these dependencies declared, and tell the analysis about them in one of the following ways:

* `usedDependencies` forces a dependency to count as used: `<usedDependencies><usedDependency>org.postgresql:postgresql</usedDependency></usedDependencies>`.
* `ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependencies` hides the "declared but unused" warning for the matching dependencies. The pattern can select by scope, for example `org.acme:::*:runtime`.
* `ignoreUnusedRuntime` ignores all runtime-scoped dependencies for the "declared but unused" check, and `ignoreNonCompile` ignores all runtime, provided, test and system-scoped dependencies.

The reverse case is a test that uses a class from a provided or runtime dependency that reaches the project only transitively. The analysis reports it as "used undeclared", and the dependency belongs in the project's POM in `test` scope. The `ignoredNonTestScopedDependencies` list is for the different case where a `compile`-scoped dependency is used by tests only and is deliberately kept in that scope.

Note that the `dependency:analyze-only` goal is used in preference to `dependency:analyze` since it doesn't force a further compilation of the project, but uses the compiled classes produced from the earlier `test-compile` phase in the lifecycle.

The project's dependencies will then be automatically analyzed during the `verify` lifecycle phase, which can be executed explicitly as follows:

```shell
mvn verify
```
